\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {13.5}Turnover, net returns and break-even costs}{188}{section.13.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:17-turnover}{{13.5}{188}{Turnover, net returns and break-even costs}{section.13.5}{}}
\newlabel{eq:17-net}{{13.1}{188}{Turnover, net returns and break-even costs}{equation.13.5.1}{}}
\newlabel{eq:17-breakeven}{{13.2}{189}{Turnover, net returns and break-even costs}{equation.13.5.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.2}{\ignorespaces The more a strategy trades, the faster its profit disappears as costs rise. Net return against one-way trading cost, from the turnover and gross returns in the table. Each line falls at a rate of four times the strategy's turnover; it crosses zero at the break-even cost. Value's line is nearly flat and crosses zero off the chart, at about 4\%.}}{189}{figure.caption.49}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:17-breakeven}{{13.2}{189}{The more a strategy trades, the faster its profit disappears as costs rise. Net return against one-way trading cost, from the turnover and gross returns in the table. Each line falls at a rate of four times the strategy's turnover; it crosses zero at the break-even cost. Value's line is nearly flat and crosses zero off the chart, at about 4\%}{figure.caption.49}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {13.6}Market impact and capacity}{190}{section.13.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:17-capacity}{{13.6}{190}{Market impact and capacity}{section.13.6}{}}
\newlabel{eq:17-impact}{{13.3}{190}{Market impact and capacity}{equation.13.6.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.3}{\ignorespaces Bigger orders cost more per dollar, though less than in proportion to their size. Square-root market impact for a stock with 2\% daily volatility, from Equation~\eqref  {eq:17-impact} with $Y = 1$. Ten times the order size costs about three times as much per dollar, not ten times; but total impact cost, per dollar times dollars, still rises faster than the order.}}{190}{figure.caption.50}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:17-impact}{{13.3}{190}{Bigger orders cost more per dollar, though less than in proportion to their size. Square-root market impact for a stock with 2\% daily volatility, from Equation~\eqref {eq:17-impact} with $Y = 1$. Ten times the order size costs about three times as much per dollar, not ten times; but total impact cost, per dollar times dollars, still rises faster than the order}{figure.caption.50}{}}
